This is a no-markup-markup source made up of some blocks.

-	This is a bullet block.

	-	This is a bullet block within a bullet block.

	-	This is another bullet block within a bullet block, and it
		contains a line break.

-	This is another bullet block. It is made up of two text blocks.

	The second text block contains an escaped character, namely an
	escaped left bracket: \[PAR:a_par]. Had the bracket not been
	escaped then ‘\[PAR:a_par]’ would have been treated as a
	cross-reference.

-	Sometimes one might want to use ‘\’. Sometimes it escapes the
	next character. Then one uses ‘\\\\’, for example if one for one
	reason want to produce ‘\\[PAR:something]’, in which case
	one uses ‘\\\\[PAR:something]’.

-	The following usage of a left bracket does not require escaping:
	f[α,β]---this because ‘f[α,β]’ is never valid syntax for a
	cross-reference and thus need not be disambiguated from such.
	Neither is the simpler ‘f[a]’---cross-references require such a
	specific syntax that very seldom---hopefully never except from
	when using nmm to describe nmm---neeeds to escape left brackets.

\¶ If for some reason one would want to begin a
level 0 text block with ‘¶’, then that is possible as well, by using
‘\\¶’.
